From ed8fc5c0aef4b8ad41e6f2fa9e4e7d7fafe8f919 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: terminills <tdeters@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 22:15:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 126/638] [AROS] Retain partial page tails before MMU backing

AI_PHASE: chromium-headless-phase-3
AI_STATUS: transition-fix
ROOT_CAUSE: Hosted run 0126 proved V8 ReadOnlyPageMetadata::ShrinkToHighWaterMark releases a valid suffix of a tracked reservation. The pre-MMU AROS backend can only free the original contiguous physical block, so treating that suffix as an exact allocation crossed allocator ownership and previously crashed kernel mm_Free.
FIX_REASON: Classify exact final frees separately from suffix releases. Shorten the allocation's logical exposed range for a suffix while retaining its original physical backing, then release the complete backing when the remaining exact range is finally freed. Prefix, middle, and foreign releases remain fail-closed with debug.resource evidence.
SCOPE: base/allocator/partition_allocator/src/partition_alloc/page_allocator_internals_aros.h
VALIDATION: AROS PartitionAlloc page_allocator.o cross-compiled; run 0126 captured the 241664-byte suffix request through gin::PageAllocator::ReleasePages -> V8 VirtualMemory::Release -> ReadOnlyPageMetadata::ShrinkToHighWaterMark.
RISK: Released suffixes continue consuming physical memory until final allocation teardown. MMU-backed sparse unmap should replace this retention policy once AROS can remove physical pages while preserving the virtual reservation.
AI_DECISION_CHAIN: run 0125 kernel allocator crash -> run 0126 fail-closed caller capture -> valid V8 suffix release -> retain physical tail with logical shrink
